09_gnn_baseline.py — Graph Neural Network (GNN) Benchmarking: ChemProp D-MPNN, 5x5 repeated CV
Log file: /home/andrey/Projects/__GITHUB__aavzpy2023/analysispaperKarimova2025/logs/09_gnn_baseline.log
=========================================================================================================

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
[EXPLAIN] What this step does
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  Compares the classical machine-learning models (steps 01/04, which describe each molecule with
  fixed fingerprints or         descriptors) against a Graph Neural Network. ChemProp's D-MPNN
  treats the molecule as a graph (atoms = nodes, bonds =         edges) and learns its own
  representation by passing messages between neighbouring atoms, instead of using a         hand-
  designed fingerprint.
  Same evaluation protocol as the classical models: 5 x 5 repeated cross-validation (data split
  into 5 parts, each part         used once as unseen test data, whole procedure repeated 5 times
  with different splits). Out-of-fold predictions are         collected so every molecule is
  predicted by a model that never saw it.
  Why it matters: with only a few hundred compounds, deep models often do not beat well-tuned
  classical models. If         ChemProp's R2 is not clearly above the classical R2 (taking the 95%
  CI into account), the simpler models are preferable         because they are faster, easier to
  interpret and not worse in accuracy. R2: 1 = perfect, 0 = no better than the mean.         MAE
  is the average error in pIC50 units (0.5 = about 3-fold in IC50).
